1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,950
Learning Nippon-go:

2
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:07,800
The Occupation's Japanese-Language
Drive, 1942 to 1945

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:11,230
On the 21st of February
1942, six days after

4
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:15,139
the British surrender,
page 4 of the Syonan Times

5
00:00:15,219 --> 00:00:18,650
carried a small box
headed "Nippon Lesson".

6
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:22,096
It listed eight everyday
phrases and how to count

7
00:00:22,176 --> 00:00:24,831
from one to twenty-one: "Good morning:

8
00:00:24,911 --> 00:00:28,725
Ohayo gozaimasu", "Thank
you: Arigato", "How are you?

9
00:00:28,725 --> 00:00:30,650
Ikaga desu?".

10
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:34,743
The paper had been the Straits
Times until a week earlier,

11
00:00:34,823 --> 00:00:40,075
and its printer and publisher was now
the Japanese novelist Ibuse Masuji.

12
00:00:40,075 --> 00:00:43,625
It was the first of hundreds of lessons.

13
00:00:43,625 --> 00:00:47,647
For the next three and a half
years the military administration

14
00:00:47,727 --> 00:00:52,010
tried to make Japanese the common
language of Singapore and Malaya,

15
00:00:52,090 --> 00:00:55,526
through schools, newspapers,
radio, campaigns and pay,

16
00:00:55,606 --> 00:01:00,750
and the newspapers of the time show both
how hard it pushed and how far it got.

17
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:03,072
The lessons appeared almost every

18
00:01:03,152 --> 00:01:05,700
day and were numbered as they went.

19
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:09,109
Lesson 10 ran on the 4th of
March, and by the 20th of

20
00:01:09,189 --> 00:01:12,993
March the paper had reached
Lesson 24, a list of words that

21
00:01:13,073 --> 00:01:16,284
ran from "Christmas:
kurisumasu" and "sore throat:

22
00:01:16,364 --> 00:01:20,234
nodo kataru" to "ticket: kippu",
each with its pronunciation

23
00:01:20,314 --> 00:01:24,000
spelled out for English readers
("koo-ree-soo-mah-soo").

24
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,139
On the 26th of February a Syonan
Times reporter wrote that the

25
00:01:28,219 --> 00:01:32,767
lessons were "proving to be a regular
feature in many Syonan homes",

26
00:01:32,847 --> 00:01:37,122
and that households where only
English and Malay had been spoken

27
00:01:37,202 --> 00:01:41,625
were picking up phrases such as
"Ohayo gozaimasu" and "Sayonara".

28
00:01:41,625 --> 00:01:45,235
The children of one family
had run up to him calling

29
00:01:45,315 --> 00:01:49,075
"Ikaga desu ka?", and he
had not known how to answer.

30
00:01:49,075 --> 00:01:52,288
Their father told him, "I
think it is very useful

31
00:01:52,368 --> 00:01:55,325
for my children to learn
a bit of Nipponese.

32
00:01:55,325 --> 00:01:59,750
It might assist them in later
life to obtain good jobs."

33
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:04,449
Ibuse had been conscripted into
the Japanese army's propaganda

34
00:02:04,529 --> 00:02:08,689
unit in November 1941 and
arrived in Singapore with it,

35
00:02:08,769 --> 00:02:13,625
and he was in charge of the
English paper for about two months.

36
00:02:13,625 --> 00:02:17,623
He later became one of
Japan's best known writers,

37
00:02:17,703 --> 00:02:23,575
the author of Black Rain (1965), a
novel about the bombing of Hiroshima.

38
00:02:23,575 --> 00:02:28,075
The policy behind the
lessons was set out in Tokyo.

39
00:02:28,075 --> 00:02:31,937
In March 1942 the Japanese
government laid down that

40
00:02:32,017 --> 00:02:36,258
education in the occupied
south should spread Japanese as

41
00:02:36,338 --> 00:02:41,110
the lingua franca of the Greater
East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere,

42
00:02:41,190 --> 00:02:44,525
the bloc of territories
Japan meant to lead.

43
00:02:44,525 --> 00:02:48,377
English was the language of
the old colonial government,

44
00:02:48,457 --> 00:02:51,125
and the new rulers wanted it replaced.

45
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:55,237
On the 4th of June 1942 the
front page of the Syonan

46
00:02:55,317 --> 00:02:58,864
Times carried a banner
across its full width,

47
00:02:58,944 --> 00:03:02,975
"Manabe Tukae Nippon-go",
Learn and Use Nippon-go.

48
00:03:02,975 --> 00:03:06,977
A poster of the period carried
the same message in Malay,

49
00:03:07,057 --> 00:03:10,558
Hindustani and Chinese,
showing a boy in a songkok

50
00:03:10,638 --> 00:03:14,075
reading from a book titled
"Nippongo, Volume 1".

51
00:03:14,075 --> 00:03:18,109
On the 1st of July 1942
the Syonan Times reported

52
00:03:18,189 --> 00:03:22,476
on the city's schools under
the Education Department

53
00:03:22,556 --> 00:03:26,250
of the Syonan Tokubetu-si,
the municipality.

54
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:30,847
There were 51 Futu Ka Gakko
(common public schools),

55
00:03:30,927 --> 00:03:33,175
with 29 more to be added.

56
00:03:33,175 --> 00:03:38,482
The existing schools included
22 Malay schools with more than

57
00:03:38,562 --> 00:03:43,250
4,000 pupils and 23 former
English schools with 3,840,

58
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:49,600
and the additions included 16 Chinese
schools and eight Indian schools.

59
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:54,156
Nippon-go was a compulsory subject,
and "English has been discarded

60
00:03:54,236 --> 00:03:58,516
as a subject from all Syonan
schools", though it was still used

61
00:03:58,596 --> 00:04:03,025
to teach "only when Malay or
Chinese fail to meet requirements".

62
00:04:03,025 --> 00:04:05,700
The paper also noted that some parents

63
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,825
were still keeping their children at home.

64
00:04:08,825 --> 00:04:12,057
Teachers had to learn the
language too, and those who

65
00:04:12,138 --> 00:04:15,575
struggled with it or would
not learn it were dismissed.

66
00:04:15,575 --> 00:04:19,919
In June 1942 the Syonan
Broadcasting Station started daily

67
00:04:19,999 --> 00:04:24,496
Nippon-go courses for the
schools, and the newspaper printed

68
00:04:24,576 --> 00:04:29,000
each day's radio lesson so
that listeners could follow it.

69
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,109
The administration also
required people to bow towards

70
00:04:33,189 --> 00:04:36,756
the Imperial Palace in
Tokyo, sing the Japanese

71
00:04:36,836 --> 00:04:41,025
national anthem and observe
Japan's national holidays.

72
00:04:41,025 --> 00:04:45,101
The main language school was
the Syonan Nippon Gakuen,

73
00:04:45,181 --> 00:04:48,875
which took students of
all ages and occupations.

74
00:04:48,875 --> 00:04:55,264
It opened on the 1st of May 1942 in
Queen Street under the poet Jinbo Kotaro,

75
00:04:55,344 --> 00:04:59,125
another member of the
army's propaganda unit.

76
00:04:59,125 --> 00:05:01,888
By early June about 100 boys and

77
00:05:01,968 --> 00:05:04,900
girls were attending its classes.

78
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:09,230
Its first graduation ceremony
was held on the 2nd of

79
00:05:09,310 --> 00:05:14,488
August 1942 at the Dai Toa
Gekizyo, the former Cathay Theatre,

80
00:05:14,568 --> 00:05:18,220
and the paper reported
that 135 students had

81
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:22,625
scored more than 85 marks
in the final examination.

82
00:05:22,625 --> 00:05:25,750
The courses were short and popular.

83
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:30,641
When the school tested applicants
for its one-month third

84
00:05:30,721 --> 00:05:35,088
term in October 1942, 234
were selected out of 734,

85
00:05:35,168 --> 00:05:40,400
and it opened a special class
for older unemployed students.

86
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:44,387
At the end of October the
school was taken over by the

87
00:05:44,467 --> 00:05:49,283
military administration and renamed
the Gunseikanbu Kokugo Gakko,

88
00:05:49,363 --> 00:05:53,652
and in 1944 the Nippon-go
School in Queen Street was still

89
00:05:53,732 --> 00:05:58,025
taking in 300 students at a
time for three-month courses.

90
00:05:58,025 --> 00:06:01,900
The administration also
paid people to learn.

91
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:06,324
In September 1942 the municipality
announced a bonus scheme for

92
00:06:06,404 --> 00:06:10,829
employees who studied Japanese,
and in November 1943 the Syonan

93
00:06:10,909 --> 00:06:14,618
Sinbun reported a revised
scheme of monthly Nippon-go

94
00:06:14,698 --> 00:06:19,266
allowances for local staff of the
military administration and the

95
00:06:19,346 --> 00:06:23,850
provincial governments who had
served for more than six months.

96
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:27,966
The allowance was up to $50 a
month for those whose Japanese

97
00:06:28,046 --> 00:06:32,162
was judged better than a
higher-school graduate's, up to $30

98
00:06:32,242 --> 00:06:36,288
for those above a primary-school
level, up to $10 for those

99
00:06:36,368 --> 00:06:40,694
who had passed the higher standard
in the Nippon-go examination

100
00:06:40,774 --> 00:06:44,900
and up to $5 for those who had
passed the intermediate one.

101
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,475
The examinations themselves were graded.

102
00:06:48,475 --> 00:06:53,255
In February 1945 tests at primary,
middle and higher level (Syokyu,

103
00:06:53,335 --> 00:06:56,592
Chukyu and Jyokyu) were
held throughout Malaya

104
00:06:56,672 --> 00:07:01,025
under new rules, and a special
grade, Tokkyu, was announced.

105
00:07:01,025 --> 00:07:03,700
There were campaigns as well.

106
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:07,609
The first Nippon-go
Popularizing Week ran for

107
00:07:07,689 --> 00:07:10,624
the first seven days of June 1942,

108
00:07:10,704 --> 00:07:15,225
and the second opened on
the 14th of November 1943.

109
00:07:15,225 --> 00:07:19,160
For that week the paper asked
its readers to "speak Nippon-Go

110
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:23,110
on every occasion", suggested
that employers arrange picnics

111
00:07:23,190 --> 00:07:27,520
or parties "at which conversation
should be entirely in Nippon-Go",

112
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:30,614
and proposed Nippon-go
speaking societies whose

113
00:07:30,694 --> 00:07:34,775
members would pay a fine whenever
they spoke another language.

114
00:07:34,775 --> 00:07:38,433
It also gave some practical
advice: "Do not try to

115
00:07:38,513 --> 00:07:42,400
master a Nippon-Go book
within a day or even a week.

116
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:46,550
Learn and master five
Nippon-Go words daily."

117
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:49,958
Small notices in the paper
that month read "Make

118
00:07:50,038 --> 00:07:53,525
It A Rule To Speak Nippon-Go
An Hour Every Day".

119
00:07:53,525 --> 00:07:57,775
By the end, the Syonan
Shimbun was claiming success.

120
00:07:57,775 --> 00:08:02,403
A column in June 1945 called the
local people "born linguists"

121
00:08:02,483 --> 00:08:06,959
and said that most of the younger
people working in Japanese

122
00:08:07,039 --> 00:08:11,975
firms and offices now spoke "quite
correct and decent Nippon-Go".

123
00:08:11,975 --> 00:08:15,699
The same column admitted
that "a very small percentage

124
00:08:15,779 --> 00:08:18,939
of the Nippon-Go speaking
local people has sat

125
00:08:19,019 --> 00:08:21,757
for the various Nippon-Go examinations",

126
00:08:21,837 --> 00:08:26,275
and blamed "either puerile
awkwardness or too adult indolence".

127
00:08:26,275 --> 00:08:30,828
Three and a half years was a short
time to change the language of a city,

128
00:08:30,908 --> 00:08:33,747
there were never enough
teachers or textbooks,

129
00:08:33,827 --> 00:08:38,650
and most people went on using their own
languages at home and in the market.

130
00:08:38,650 --> 00:08:44,113
The Syonan Shimbun printed its last
issue on the 4th of September 1945,

131
00:08:44,193 --> 00:08:48,800
as British forces arrived, and
the lessons stopped with it.

132
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:52,550
The newspaper lessons
did more than list words.

133
00:08:52,550 --> 00:08:55,966
By Lesson 103, printed
on the 23rd of June 1942,

134
00:08:56,046 --> 00:08:59,825
they had reached grammar,
with a note that adjectives

135
00:08:59,905 --> 00:09:03,904
come before the nouns they
describe and that "ka" placed

136
00:09:03,984 --> 00:09:07,625
at the end of a sentence
turns it into a question.

137
00:09:07,625 --> 00:09:11,996
At the start of 1986, straight
after graduating from NUS,

138
00:09:12,076 --> 00:09:16,447
I went to Japan for 18 months
on a scholarship from AOTS,

139
00:09:16,527 --> 00:09:21,991
a Japanese organisation that trains
engineers and managers from abroad,

140
00:09:22,071 --> 00:09:27,225
and we started with Nihongo lessons
at the Yokohama Kenshu Center.

141
00:09:27,225 --> 00:09:32,150
I was not a good student, and it
was years before I understood why.

142
00:09:32,150 --> 00:09:34,754
The teaching was intensive, but no one

143
00:09:34,834 --> 00:09:38,225
explained how differently
the language is built.

144
00:09:38,225 --> 00:09:41,910
Japanese puts the verb at the
end of a sentence (subject,

145
00:09:41,990 --> 00:09:45,872
object, verb), while English
puts it in the middle (subject,

146
00:09:45,952 --> 00:09:48,712
verb, object), and I
wasn't sharp enough to

147
00:09:48,792 --> 00:09:52,425
work out that basic but
critical difference for myself.

148
00:09:52,425 --> 00:09:57,248
I still picked up a smattering of
spoken Japanese from daily life,

149
00:09:57,328 --> 00:10:00,225
but I count it as a wasted opportunity.

150
00:10:00,225 --> 00:10:04,333
Why it matters today: The
Occupation tried to make Singapore

151
00:10:04,413 --> 00:10:08,939
speak Japanese by order, through
compulsory lessons, examinations,

152
00:10:09,019 --> 00:10:12,918
allowances and slogans, and
its own newspaper conceded at

153
00:10:12,998 --> 00:10:17,325
the end that only a small share
of speakers had sat its tests.

154
00:10:17,325 --> 00:10:21,546
Japanese is still taught in
Singapore today, but by choice:

155
00:10:21,626 --> 00:10:25,337
since 1978 the Ministry of
Education Language Centre

156
00:10:25,417 --> 00:10:30,002
has offered it as a third language
to secondary school students,

157
00:10:30,082 --> 00:10:33,800
and many people learn it
for work, study or travel.

158
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:37,760
The two show the difference
between a language that people are

159
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:42,075
made to learn and one they choose
to learn for their own reasons.
